1
00:00:00,193 --> 00:00:01,633
Rosa needs a refill.

2
00:00:02,906 --> 00:00:07,513
She taps Refill My Prescription in her
pharmacy's mobile app, which triggers an

3
00:00:07,513 --> 00:00:08,272
agentic flow.

4
00:00:10,266 --> 00:00:16,742
Under the hood. The agentic flow is a real
app, running on rossoctl, an agent platform on

5
00:00:16,742 --> 00:00:17,542
Kubernetes.

6
00:00:17,842 --> 00:00:21,345
Five agents and eight tools, each in its own
pod.

7
00:00:21,645 --> 00:00:26,329
The pharmacy assistant takes Rosa's request.
It looks her up, and asks for a clinical

8
00:00:26,329 --> 00:00:29,360
review, which saves the fill to the pharmacy's
records.

9
00:00:29,660 --> 00:00:34,581
Then fulfillment books the courier, and
reports the refill to an outside company: the

10
00:00:34,581 --> 00:00:36,376
wholesaler's demand forecaster.

11
00:00:39,462 --> 00:00:43,462
That one tap sets off 26 agent and tool
interactions.

12
00:00:44,781 --> 00:00:48,996
Sentry tracks the data through each one and
checks it against company policy.

13
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:53,008
One it marks as risky, let's see why.

14
00:00:54,837 --> 00:01:00,624
This is Sentry's console, Data Governance,
recording Rosa's tap as it happens. Sped up.

15
00:01:02,704 --> 00:01:09,590
Each node is an agent, a tool, the model, or a
vendor. Each arrow is one exchange: a request

16
00:01:09,590 --> 00:01:10,637
and its reply.

17
00:01:10,937 --> 00:01:16,582
The count changes a little from run to run. In
this one, Sentry counted 28 interactions for

18
00:01:16,582 --> 00:01:18,939
the tap, 8 of them calls to the model.

19
00:01:19,239 --> 00:01:22,742
Open any exchange, and Sentry shows who called
whom,

20
00:01:23,042 --> 00:01:28,980
and exactly what was sent: here, her name and
birth date, going to the patient lookup.

21
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:34,357
With the risk layer on, one exchange turns
red: the report to the forecaster.

22
00:01:37,374 --> 00:01:42,473
Sentry is data governance on top of Cortex,
which monitors the traffic between agents and

23
00:01:42,473 --> 00:01:43,733
everything they touch.

24
00:01:44,491 --> 00:01:48,070
It's built on lineage, classification, and
risk assessment.

25
00:01:49,995 --> 00:01:55,942
How does Sentry see all this? Cortex runs a
sidecar, a small proxy, beside every agent and

26
00:01:55,942 --> 00:01:58,651
tool. There is no Sentry code in the app.

27
00:01:58,951 --> 00:02:04,480
For every exchange, the sidecar sends Sentry
two records: the request and the response,

28
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:05,751
with their payloads.

29
00:02:06,051 --> 00:02:09,199
From these, Sentry rebuilds who called whom.

30
00:02:09,499 --> 00:02:15,349
Classification reads every payload and names
each value in it: a person's name, a medical

31
00:02:15,349 --> 00:02:16,795
record number, a date.

32
00:02:17,095 --> 00:02:22,099
Lineage follows each value from exchange to
exchange, and its label travels with it.

33
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:27,326
Risk assessment checks every exchange against
the company policy, and records a verdict.

34
00:02:30,609 --> 00:02:33,117
Let's take one rule from the company policy.

35
00:02:33,970 --> 00:02:36,897
Personal data must not leave the organization.

36
00:02:39,308 --> 00:02:43,163
In Sentry, the company policy is written down
as three rules.

37
00:02:43,463 --> 00:02:50,091
Rule DG-001: personal data, sent to an
untrusted external destination. Its suggested

38
00:02:50,091 --> 00:02:51,748
enforcement is block.

39
00:02:52,048 --> 00:02:57,698
Rule DG-002 says the same for health data, and
DG-004 for restricted data.

40
00:02:57,998 --> 00:03:03,848
Untrusted means outside the organization. The
cluster's own services count as inside. Here

41
00:03:03,848 --> 00:03:06,643
the contracted courier runs as one of them.

42
00:03:06,943 --> 00:03:09,805
So does any address on a list the company
sets.

43
00:03:10,105 --> 00:03:14,606
The forecaster's address is on neither:
external, and untrusted.

44
00:03:14,906 --> 00:03:22,136
Sentry evaluates the rules with OPA, the Open
Policy Agent. Here is rule DG-001, in Rego, as

45
00:03:22,136 --> 00:03:24,337
OPA runs it in this cluster.

46
00:03:24,637 --> 00:03:30,764
Any flow that carries an item tagged PII, to a
destination that is external and untrusted,

47
00:03:30,764 --> 00:03:31,785
fires the rule.

48
00:03:35,150 --> 00:03:36,157
Level one.

49
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:40,096
In Rosa's record, the context is all there.

50
00:03:40,637 --> 00:03:43,785
Her name and beside it her medical record
number.

51
00:03:44,911 --> 00:03:49,918
Both are personal data and the vendor is
external outside the organization.

52
00:03:50,726 --> 00:03:54,802
Sending them there breaks the policy, so
Sentry recommends blocking it.

53
00:03:55,638 --> 00:03:59,854
With full context at the point of decision,
this is local enforcement.

54
00:04:00,527 --> 00:04:03,956
Most data governance tools can do it, and so
does Sentry.

55
00:04:05,769 --> 00:04:11,061
Level one, in Sentry: the report to the
forecaster, from Rosa's refill.

56
00:04:11,361 --> 00:04:15,570
Its payload holds her name, her birth date,
and her medical record number.

57
00:04:15,870 --> 00:04:21,798
Classification reads it. A trained model finds
each value and names its type, and a catalog

58
00:04:21,798 --> 00:04:24,729
gives each type its tags and its sensitivity.

59
00:04:25,029 --> 00:04:30,452
Her name is found right here: personal data,
restricted. A check that sees only this

60
00:04:30,452 --> 00:04:31,679
message has enough.

61
00:04:31,979 --> 00:04:37,957
So the input Sentry sends to OPA reads:
personal data, restricted, going to an

62
00:04:37,957 --> 00:04:40,409
external, untrusted destination.

63
00:04:40,709 --> 00:04:48,207
Rules DG-001 and DG-004 fire on what is found
here. DG-002, for health data, fires on the

64
00:04:48,207 --> 00:04:50,566
number: level two shows why.

65
00:04:50,866 --> 00:04:56,549
OPA combines the rules, most restrictive
first: risk level critical, suggested

66
00:04:56,549 --> 00:04:57,861
enforcement block.

67
00:04:58,161 --> 00:05:03,236
Sentry raises an alert and recommends the
block. In this setup it does not hold the

68
00:05:03,236 --> 00:05:05,437
call: the report still goes through.

69
00:05:05,736 --> 00:05:11,259
The courier gets her name and address too. It
runs inside the organization, so that call is

70
00:05:11,259 --> 00:05:11,745
allowed.

71
00:05:14,769 --> 00:05:17,056
But what happens when the context is gone?

72
00:05:18,635 --> 00:05:21,926
Level two. The app now sends only the number.

73
00:05:22,759 --> 00:05:28,569
At release, it's out of context, so given only
the local context, it's classified as a

74
00:05:28,569 --> 00:05:30,123
generic operational ID.

75
00:05:31,582 --> 00:05:33,663
Local enforcement would let it through.

76
00:05:34,215 --> 00:05:37,357
But Sentry's lineage carries the context with
the data.

77
00:05:37,955 --> 00:05:41,676
Four hops back, that number was Rosa's medical
record number.

78
00:05:42,808 --> 00:05:48,315
It's still personal data, so sending it breaks
the policy, and Sentry recommends blocking it.

79
00:05:50,051 --> 00:05:52,911
With full context, Sentry recommends a block.

80
00:05:53,382 --> 00:05:57,671
Four hops downstream and out of context, it
still catches the leak.

81
00:05:59,724 --> 00:06:04,304
Level two, in Sentry. The report now sends
only the number.

82
00:06:04,604 --> 00:06:10,864
Read alone, the classifier calls it an
operational ID: internal. No rule fires on

83
00:06:10,864 --> 00:06:11,250
that.

84
00:06:11,550 --> 00:06:16,220
Now track that number on the graph. Every
exchange that carried it lights up, the

85
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:17,200
model's included.

86
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:22,978
Hop one. The patient lookup answers the
assistant: her name and her medical record

87
00:06:22,978 --> 00:06:25,716
number, together. Here the label is born.

88
00:06:26,016 --> 00:06:29,663
Hop two. The assistant passes the number to
fulfillment.

89
00:06:29,963 --> 00:06:33,463
Hop three. Fulfillment passes it to the report
tool.

90
00:06:33,763 --> 00:06:37,052
Hop four. The report sends it to the
forecaster.

91
00:06:37,563 --> 00:06:43,277
At every hop, Sentry matches the exact value,
so the label, medical record number, travels

92
00:06:43,277 --> 00:06:43,785
with it.

93
00:06:44,085 --> 00:06:50,520
OPA now gets the carried label. The same three
rules fire: critical, recommend block.

94
00:06:53,141 --> 00:06:56,217
But what if the leak comes from a different
session altogether?

95
00:06:57,310 --> 00:07:02,453
A nightly job pulls the number from a shared
database, with no name and no context.

96
00:07:03,746 --> 00:07:08,955
Sentry's cross-session lineage carries the
full context across sessions, so this leak is

97
00:07:08,955 --> 00:07:09,606
caught too.

98
00:07:11,752 --> 00:07:17,517
Level three, in Sentry. The nightly job runs
as a session of its own, with its own trace.

99
00:07:17,517 --> 00:07:20,886
In this demo it runs a few minutes after the
refill.

100
00:07:21,186 --> 00:07:26,457
The shared database is the pharmacy's records
service. During Rosa's refill, it saved the

101
00:07:26,457 --> 00:07:27,760
fill under her number.

102
00:07:28,060 --> 00:07:33,504
At night, the batch reads the day's fills from
the records. Read alone, the number in that

103
00:07:33,504 --> 00:07:35,560
answer is an operational ID again.

104
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:40,528
Lineage reaches back across sessions: the
records got that number in Rosa's refill,

105
00:07:40,528 --> 00:07:45,083
where it came from the patient lookup, next to
her name. The label comes with it.

106
00:07:45,383 --> 00:07:51,211
Reading the records stays inside, so that call
is allowed. The risk is the next call: the

107
00:07:51,211 --> 00:07:56,776
batch's export to the forecaster. Critical,
recommend block, on the same three rules,

108
00:07:56,776 --> 00:07:58,675
linked back to Rosa's refill.

109
00:08:01,931 --> 00:08:05,149
Sentry doesn't fix the app, it applies the
policy.

110
00:08:06,229 --> 00:08:11,745
Once the app is fixed so it no longer releases
personal data, sending the vendor only what it

111
00:08:11,745 --> 00:08:13,524
needs, Sentry lets it through.

112
00:08:15,305 --> 00:08:21,308
The fixed app, in Sentry. The report now sends
the drug and the dates. No patient key.

113
00:08:21,608 --> 00:08:27,044
Nothing in it is personal data, so no rule
fires: allowed, no alert.

114
00:08:27,345 --> 00:08:30,565
The nightly batch is fixed the same way.
Allowed too.

115
00:08:30,865 --> 00:08:35,444
The fix is in the app. Sentry found the leak,
and it confirms the fix.

116
00:08:38,274 --> 00:08:43,584
This way, Rosa gets her refill, and the policy
holds, keeping the company in line with data

117
00:08:43,584 --> 00:08:44,926
protection regulations.

118
00:08:47,906 --> 00:08:50,125
Local enforcement sees one message.

119
00:08:51,029 --> 00:08:56,832
Sentry sees its whole history, protecting data
locally, within a session and across session

120
00:08:56,832 --> 00:08:57,533
boundaries.

121
00:08:59,379 --> 00:09:06,209
Every verdict in this film is Sentry's own,
from one run on rossoctl: 5 sessions, 102

122
00:09:06,209 --> 00:09:08,459
exchanges judged, 3 flagged.

123
00:09:11,097 --> 00:09:15,028
Sentry, with lineage and risk, is live in the
next release.
